Cloudberry's gp_stats_collector tests on the port's cluster and on one node:
the differences from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed,
and are the port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from, with .single
before .diff for the one node, and .planner or .orca for one pass alone; a
difference that depends on the run has a file for each way it may come
out, .<n> before .diff.  run.sh counts a test as passing when gpdiff.pl,
under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's greenplum one, finds no
difference or exactly one of the files'.

gpsc_utility, gpsc_utility.single  CREATE TABLE with Cloudberry's classic
  partition clause: the port makes each partition a CREATE TABLE ...
  PARTITION OF of its own, run as a subcommand of the statement, with its
  text (gp_sql's partition.c), which the collector reports as a nested
  utility statement, as it reports PostgreSQL's own subcommands -- a
  primary key's index -- in Cloudberry too; Cloudberry's grammar made the
  partitions within the one statement.  Two partitions: two more pairs of
  SUBMIT and DONE.

gpsc_dist.planner.0, .1, .2  A replicated table joined with a function
  EXECUTE ON ALL SEGMENTS: the port's planner reads the function on every
  segment and the table on one, the session's (gp_scan.c's
  GpScanReplicatedContent(), its process id modulo the segments), and
  joins them on the coordinator, so that segment runs one query more than
  Cloudberry's one slice, and reports it: four more rows, under the
  segment of the run -- 0, whose segment id the log leaves empty, as a
  protobuf leaves a zero out, 1 or 2.

gpsc_dist.orca  The same query: ORCA's plan has two slices on each segment
  -- the function's rows redistributed into the slice that joins them with
  the table -- where Cloudberry's has one, and a segment reports each slice
  it runs, as Cloudberry's does: four more rows for each segment.
